 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Orthogonality:** Naive aggregation of raw SH polynomials is futile because the integral of any purely high-order SH polynomial ($l \ge 1$) over the sphere evaluates to strictly zero due to orthogonality [S1].
-*   **Stability:** The final linear layer of the enhancement MLP $\Phi$ must be strictly zero-initialized to ensure optimization stability, allowing the model to fall back to standard 3DGS at the start of training [S1].
-*   **High-Order Inefficiency:** High-order SH polynomials are inefficient for sharp specular lobes due to their dense and global nature [S1].
+*   **Orthogonality:** Naive aggregation of raw SH polynomials is futile because the integral of any purely high-order SH polynomial ($l \ge 1$) over the sphere is zero [S1]. The method uses the residual between orders to avoid this.
+*   **Stability:** The final linear layer of $\Phi$ must be zero-initialized to allow the model to fall back to standard 3DGS at the start of training [S1].
+*   **Position Normalization:** Positions outside the unit sphere are normalized using a specific projection to reduce the impact of spatial outliers [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The aggregation step is $O(K \cdot N)$ where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $K$ is the number of samples. This is performed once during the transition from third-order to first-order SH [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduces SH storage from third-order (16 coefficients per channel) to first-order (4 coefficients per channel) plus the overhead of the MLP parameters and aggregated latents [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Flux-GS achieves PSNR comparable to 3DGS and Mobile-GS while reducing the number of Gaussian primitives and facilitating significantly higher FPS on mobile hardware (Snapdragon 8 Gen 3 GPU) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** No pre-training or distillation required; zero inference overhead for the enhancement module (offsets are baked in); reduces storage costs and Gaussian point requirements [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires an initial 3,000 iterations of third-order SH optimization before compression [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The aggregation step is $O(N \cdot K)$, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $K$ is the number of samples. With $K=2048$, this is a one-time cost during the transition from high-order to low-order training [S1]. The inference cost is $O(1)$ per Gaussian for the enhancement MLP, but since the offset is baked in, rendering is $O(N)$ [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduces SH storage from 48 floats (third-order) to 12 floats (first-order) plus 1 scalar and 9 floats for latents (if stored) or mapped directly. The paper highlights significant reduction in storage cost (e.g., 1.6 MB vs 4.2 MB for Mobile-GS) [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves PSNR comparable to 3DGS and Mobile-GS while requiring fewer Gaussian points and higher FPS on mobile hardware (Snapdragon 8 Gen 3) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** No pre-training or distillation required; zero inference overhead for enhancement; preserves specular features [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the assumption that specular energy can be captured by first-order moments; may lose very fine high-frequency details not captured by the residual [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The Monte Carlo Specular Energy Aggregator was proposed by Xiaobiao Du, YuAn Wang, Hao Li, Bosheng Wang, Xun Sun, and Xin Yu in the paper "Monte Carlo Energy Aggregation for Mobile 3D Gaussian Splatting" (Flux-GS), published in arXiv:2606.30017v1 on June 29, 2026 [S1]. It was developed to address the memory overhead of high-order Spherical Harmonics in 3D Gaussian Splatting for mobile rendering, replacing the distillation-based approach of Mobile-GS [S1].
+The method was proposed by Xiaobiao Du, YuAn Wang, Hao Li, Bosheng Wang, Xun Sun, and Xin Yu in the paper "Monte Carlo Energy Aggregation for Mobile 3D Gaussian Splatting" (Flux-GS), published in 2026 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of Mobile-GS, which uses distillation, by introducing a direct aggregation method [S1].
 
